Deliver cancel/pause/reset to the owning worker via a nullable `runs.control` column, verified end to end in the split-process check
- Verbs (cancel/move-away, pause, reset) still move the card at once in the calling process and additionally write `runs.control`; the owning worker polls it (RADULF_CONTROL_POLL_INTERVAL_MS) and fires its local AbortController, so the harness only sees the AbortSignal it already honoured.
- `src/server/splitProcesses.test.ts` now strips `RADULF_CONTROL_POLL_INTERVAL_MS` from the inherited env and sets it to 200 ms for the workers.
- New test: "cancelling a looping card from the web-only process ends the run in the worker" — a `stall` loop run that can only end via abort is cancelled from web1; asserts the run row lands `cancelled` with `control` cleared, `exit_reason` "cancelled by user", one `run.finished` event with status cancelled, all iterations failed, and the `runtmp/<runId>` scratch dir removed.
- New test: "pausing a looping card ... resume starts a new claimed run" — a `phantom` loop is paused from web1; asserts the first loop run becomes `paused` with `exit_reason` "paused by user" and `control` "pause", resume yields a second worker-claimed loop run, and the card settles before later tests assert every run has ended.
- Both tests placed between the drive test and the contention test and leave their runs finished so the disjoint-interval/ended_at assertions downstream hold.
- Checked with `npx tsc --noEmit && npx eslint src/server/splitProcesses.test.ts`; the split check itself runs only under `make check-split`.
